  "status_text": "Two lights can make the cost of a circuit's mistakes come down to covering every link. For any chosen limit on bit-flipping gates, Lean checked a test with a penalty for lighting both outputs together (L-lib-1369). Take three vertices linked in a triangle, each costing one to pick. Cover every link by picking at least one end. Two picks are needed, and the smallest possible circuit penalty is exactly 14, a baseline of 12 plus the cost of those two picks. The search can now use coverage to set exact error targets for two lights.",
